tippy('.yamadori', {
    content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; "><h3 style="margin-bottom:5px;">Copper pheasant (<ruby>山鳥<rt>やまどり</rt></ruby>, yamadori)</h3><p>【Noun】A bird native to Japan. The male is easily recognized by its long tail feathers. It was once believed that mated pairs spent the day together but slept on opposite sides of a valley at night.</p></div><img loading="lazy" src="/img/yamadori.jpg" alt="山鳥" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin:0 auto 10px;"></div>', allowHTML: true
});
tippy('.fuji_yama', {
    content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; "><h3 style="margin-bottom:5px;">Mount Fuji (<ruby>富士山<rt>ふじさん</rt></ruby>, Fuji-san)</h3><p>【Noun】Japan\'s highest mountain (3,776 m) and the country\'s most iconic peak. An active volcano located on the border of present-day Shizuoka and Yamanashi Prefectures, it has long been admired for its snow-covered summit and celebrated in Japanese poetry and art. When this poem was composed in the 8th century, Mount Fuji is believed to have still been emitting volcanic smoke.</p></div><img loading="lazy" src="/img/fujisan_shinkansen_small.webp" alt="富士山" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin:0 auto 10px;"></div>', allowHTML: true
});
tippy('.kasasagi', {
    content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column;"><h3 style="margin-bottom:5px;">Magpie (kasasagi)</h3><p><strong>Noun:</strong> A member of the crow family, slightly smaller than a crow. It is easily recognized by its striking black-and-white plumage, with glossy blue-green iridescence on its tail rather than a solid black color. In this poem, Yakamochi is thought to have likened the bird\'s white feathers to frost.</p></div><img loading="lazy" src="/img/kasasagi.jpg" alt="Magpie" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin:0 auto 10px;"></div>',
    allowHTML: true
});
tippy('.gosechinomai', {
    content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px; margin-bottom:0;"><div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column;"><h3 style="margin-bottom:5px;">The Gosechi dance (<ruby>五節<rt>ごせち</rt></ruby>の<ruby>舞<rt>まい</rt></ruby>, gosechi no mai)</h3><p><strong>Noun:</strong> A ceremonial court dance performed at the Imperial Court during important harvest festivals. Four or five unmarried young women, dressed in the elegant jūnihitoe (twelve-layer ceremonial robes), danced as an offering of gratitude for a successful rice harvest. They carried Japanese cypress fans (hiōgi) and wore ornamental combs in their hair.</p></div><img loading="lazy" src="/img/hokusai_12.webp" alt="gosechi-no-mai" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin:0 auto"></div ><div style="font-size:11px; line-height:1.2; margin:3px auto 10px; color:#fff;"> © The Trustees of the British Museum</div> ',
    allowHTML: true
});


/* ---- Poem 2 (2_en.html) ---- */
tippy('.shinkokinshu', { content: '<h3>Shin Kokin Wakashū</h3><p>The eighth imperial anthology of waka, completed in 1205 by order of Retired Emperor Go-Toba. Fujiwara no Sadaie (Teika) was one of its compilers. It contains 1,978 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_angels', { content: '<h3>"Angels of the sky"</h3><p>Porter\'s free rendering for English readers of 1909. There are no angels in the original poem: <em>Ama no Kaguyama</em> simply means "heavenly Mount Kagu," and Porter pictured heavenly beings hanging out their robes.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kaguyama', { content: '<h3>Mount Kagu (Ama no Kaguyama)</h3><p>A low sacred hill (152 m) in present-day Kashihara, Nara Prefecture, one of the Three Mountains of Yamato. It is called "heavenly" (<em>ama no</em>) because, in myth, it was said to have come down from Takamanohara, the world of the gods.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shirotae', { content: '<h3>Shirotae (白妙)</h3><p>White cloth woven from the bark fibers of the paper mulberry and similar trees, and so also "pure white." In poems, <em>shirotae no</em> is a pillow word (<em>makurakotoba</em>) for white things, such as <em>koromo</em> (robes) or snow.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hujiwarakyo', { content: '<h3>Fujiwara-kyō</h3><p>Japan\'s first capital city built on the Chinese model, with streets laid out in a grid, completed in 694 in present-day Kashihara, Nara Prefecture. The <em>Nihon Shoki</em> calls it <em>Aramashi no Miyako</em>, "the newly enlarged capital"; the name "Fujiwara-kyō" was first used by a scholar in 1913, after the name of its palace, Fujiwara Palace.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jinshinnoran', { content: '<h3>The Jinshin War (672)</h3><p>The largest civil war of ancient Japan, fought over the throne between Prince Ōama (later Emperor Tenmu) and Prince Ōtomo (Emperor Kōbun). It is named after the year 672, which was a <em>jinshin</em> year in the sixty-year cycle of the old calendar.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tenmu', { content: '<h3>Emperor Tenmu</h3><p>The 40th emperor and Empress Jitō\'s husband. His personal name was Ōama. He was a younger brother of Emperor Tenji, Empress Jitō\'s father, so he was also her uncle.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jotei', { content: '<h3>An empress who ruled</h3><p>Japan has had several women emperors who reigned in their own right. Empress Jitō was the 41st emperor. In English the word "empress" is used both for a ruler\'s wife and for a woman ruler; from 690, Empress Jitō was the ruler herself.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ritsuryouseido', { content: '<h3>Ritsuryō system</h3><p>A state run by written law codes, modeled on those of Tang China: <em>ritsu</em> (criminal law) and <em>ryō</em> (administrative and civil law).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.otsunomiko', { content: '<h3>Prince Ōtsu (663–686)</h3><p>A son of Emperor Tenmu. His mother, Princess Ōta, was Empress Jitō\'s elder sister. Right after Emperor Tenmu died in 686, he was charged with plotting a rebellion and took his own life at 24.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.iwarenoike', { content: '<h3>Iware Pond</h3><p>A pond made by the early Yamato kings. It is thought to have been around present-day Kashihara and Sakurai in Nara Prefecture.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jomei', { content: '<h3>Emperor Jomei</h3><p>The 34th emperor. He was the father of Emperor Tenji and Emperor Tenmu, and so Empress Jitō\'s grandfather.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.akizushima', { content: '<h3>Akizushima</h3><p>An old poetic name for Yamato, or for Japan. One theory says it comes from Emperor Jinmu, who looked out over Yamato and said its shape was like a dragonfly (<em>akizu</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nihonshoki', { content: '<h3>Nihon Shoki</h3><p>Japan\'s oldest official history, completed in 720. It tells the story of Japan from the age of the gods to the reign of Empress Jitō.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.gyokuza', { content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div><h3>Takamikura</h3><p>The emperor\'s throne. The photo was taken in 1928.</p></div><figure style="margin:0;"><img loading="lazy" src="/img/takamikura.webp" alt="Takamikura" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin:0 auto 10px;"><figcaption><small>Source: Naikaku Kanbō Taireishi (the Cabinet enthronement office), Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons</small></figcaption></figure></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.daigokuden', { content: '<h3>Great Hall of State (Daigokuden)</h3><p>The main hall of the palace, where the emperor attended to government and ceremonies.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.choudouin', { content: '<h3>State Halls (Chōdōin)</h3><p>The halls south of the Great Hall of State, where national ceremonies were held.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kamakura', { content: '<h3>Kamakura period</h3><p>The age of the first samurai government, based in Kamakura (c. 1185–1333).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.asukakiyomihara', { content: '<h3>Asuka Kiyomihara Palace</h3><p>The palace of Emperor Tenmu and Empress Jitō, thought to have been in present-day Asuka Village, Nara Prefecture. One theory says it was named in the hope that Emperor Tenmu would recover from an illness.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.gosenshu', { content: '<h3>Gosen Wakashū</h3><p>The second imperial anthology of waka, completed around 953 by order of Emperor Murakami. Its compilers included Kiyohara no Motosuke and Ōnakatomi no Yoshinobu. It contains 1,426 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kariho', { content: '<h3>Makeshift hut (kariho no io)</h3><p><em>Kariho</em> is a shortened form of <em>kari-io</em>, a temporary hut. Farmers kept watch from it to protect the rice from birds and animals, and pulled it down after the harvest. <em>Kariho</em> can also be heard as 刈り穂, "cut ears of rice," so the word carries both meanings (a <em>kakekotoba</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.toma', { content: '<h3>The roof mats (toma)</h3><p>Mats woven from grasses such as sedge and cogon grass, used to roof a hut.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_rain', { content: '<h3>"Wet with rain"</h3><p>Porter\'s free rendering. In the original, the sleeves are wet with the night dew (<em>tsuyu</em>), not with rain.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.manyoushu', { content: '<h3>Man\'yōshū</h3><p>Japan\'s oldest surviving collection of waka, completed in the late Nara period (8th century). Ōtomo no Yakamochi is thought to have led its compilation. It contains about 4,500 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.taikanokaishin', { content: '<h3>The Taika Reforms</h3><p>A series of political reforms that began with the Isshi Incident of 645. They changed Japan from a country run by powerful clans into one centered on the emperor.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kanmu', { content: '<h3>Emperor Kanmu</h3><p>The 50th emperor. He moved the capital to Heian-kyō (present-day Kyoto) in 794, which began the Heian period.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.heiankyo', { content: '<h3>Heian-kyō and the Heian period</h3><p>Heian-kyō, in present-day Kyoto, became the capital in 794 under Emperor Kanmu. The Heian period, named after it, lasted about 390 years, until the Kamakura shogunate was set up at the end of the 12th century.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.isshinohen', { content: '<h3>The Isshi Incident (645)</h3><p>A coup in which Prince Naka no Ōe (later Emperor Tenji), Nakatomi no Kamatari and others killed Soga no Iruka in the palace and brought down the Soga clan.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hakusukinoe', { content: '<h3>Baekgang (Hakusukinoe)</h3><p>The lower reaches of the present-day Geum River in South Korea. In Japanese it is read Hakusukinoe or Hakusonkō.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.koubun', { content: '<h3>Prince Ōtomo (Emperor Kōbun)</h3><p>The 39th emperor and Emperor Tenji\'s eldest son. His personal name was Ōtomo. He lost the Jinshin War to Prince Ōama (later Emperor Tenmu) and took his own life.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kishitsuhukushin', { content: '<h3>Kwisil Poksin</h3><p>A Baekje general. When Baekje fell in 660, he raised an army and made Puyŏ P\'ung king, but he was later killed by that king.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yohoushou', { content: '<h3>Puyŏ P\'ung</h3><p>A prince of Baekje in the late 7th century. He had been living in Japan as a hostage, and went home when Baekje fell in 660 and he was asked to return.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.toyohatagumo', { content: '<h3>Flag-like clouds (toyohatagumo)</h3><p>Long clouds that stretch across the sky like great flags waving in the wind.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shuuishu', { content: '<h3>Shūi Wakashū</h3><p>The third imperial anthology of waka, completed around 1005. It was compiled by Retired Emperor Kazan and those around him, expanding Fujiwara no Kintō\'s <em>Shūishō</em>. It contains 1,351 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ashibikino', { content: '<h3>The pillow word <em>ashibiki no</em></h3><p>In the original, <em>yama</em> (mountain) is preceded by <em>ashibiki no</em>, a pillow word (<em>makurakotoba</em>) that leads into it. Its origin is unclear: one theory says it comes from dragging one\'s feet when climbing a mountain, another from the long, trailing foot of a mountain.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sanka', { content: '<h3>Poems praising the emperor (sanka)</h3><p>Poems written to praise someone — here, the emperor and the royal family.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.banka', { content: '<h3>Poems of mourning (banka)</h3><p>Poems mourning the dead.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kasei', { content: '<h3>Sages of Poetry (kasei)</h3><p>A title for the very greatest waka poets, used above all for Kakinomoto no Hitomaro and Yamabe no Akahito. The preface of the <em>Kokin Wakashū</em> already calls Hitomaro "the sage of poetry."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sanjurokkasen', { content: '<h3>Thirty-Six Immortal Poets</h3><p>Thirty-six master poets chosen by Fujiwara no Kintō in his <em>Sanjūrokuninsen</em> (early 11th century).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kusakabe', { content: '<h3>Prince Kusakabe</h3><p>The son of Emperor Tenmu and Empress Jitō. He became crown prince in 681, but fell ill and died in 689 at just 27.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.makuranosoushi', { content: '<h3>The Pillow Book (Makura no Sōshi)</h3><p>Japan\'s oldest collection of essays, by Sei Shōnagon. It often uses the word <em>okashi</em> ("delightful, charming").</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mujoukan', { content: '<h3>Nothing lasts (mujōkan)</h3><p>The view that nothing stays the same: everything comes into being, changes, and passes away. It runs deep in Japanese literature.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.imayou', { content: '<h3>Imayō</h3><p>A kind of song made of four lines of seven and five syllables each, popular from the middle of the Heian period to the Kamakura period.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ajirogi', { content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div><h3>Ajirogi (fish-trap stakes)</h3><p>An <em>ajiro</em> was a trap of woven bamboo for catching small fish called <em>hio</em>; <em>ajirogi</em> were the stakes that held it in place. The stakes were set in the river in a V shape to lead the fish in.</p></div><figure style="margin:0 auto 10px;"><img loading="lazy" src="/img/ajiro.webp?1" alt="Ajirogi" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto;"><figcaption><small><em>Ishiyamadera Engi</em>, National Diet Library Digital Collections</small></figcaption></figure></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.setsugekka', { content: '<h3>Snow, moon and flowers (setsugekka)</h3><p>The classic beauties of nature, each at its loveliest season. The phrase comes from a line by the Chinese poet Bai Juyi, and in Japan it came to stand for the elegant enjoyment of nature through the seasons.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.narajidai', { content: '<h3>Nara period (710–794)</h3><p>The age when the capital was at Heijō-kyō, in present-day Nara, from 710 until the move to Heian-kyō in 794.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shoumu', { content: '<h3>Emperor Shōmu</h3><p>The 45th emperor, eldest son of Emperor Monmu. Hoping Buddhism would protect the country, he had provincial temples (<em>kokubunji</em>) built across Japan and Tōdai-ji built in the capital.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jokeika', { content: '<h3>Nature poetry (jokeika)</h3><p>Poems that describe scenery and nature, letting the poet\'s feelings show through the scene.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.gyoukou', { content: '<h3>Imperial journeys (gyōkō)</h3><p>A journey made by the emperor. A journey by a retired emperor or empress was called <em>gokō</em>. Both could also be read <em>miyuki</em>.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nikitatsu', { content: '<h3>Nikitatsu</h3><p>A port thought to have been near present-day Matsuyama City, Ehime Prefecture.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hisagi', { content: '<h3>Hisagi</h3><p>A tree name used in the <em>Man\'yōshū</em>. There are different views, but it is often taken to be the Japanese mallotus (<em>akamegashiwa</em>), a tree 5–10 m tall with large leaves.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sumire', { content: '<h3>Violets (sumire)</h3><p>A small wild perennial that blooms purple in spring. Its name is said to come from the shape of the flower, which looks like a carpenter\'s ink pot (<em>sumi-ire</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kokinshu', { content: '<h3>Kokin Wakashū</h3><p>The first imperial anthology of waka, presented to Emperor Daigo, who ordered it, in 905 and revised afterward. Its compilers were Ki no Tsurayuki, Ki no Tomonori, Ōshikōchi no Mitsune and Mibu no Tadamine. It contains about 1,100 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.utaawase', { content: '<h3>Poetry contest (utaawase)</h3><p>The poets were split into two teams, Left and Right. One poet from each side presented a poem in turn, and a judge decided which was better; at the end, the team results were compared to decide the winner.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.koresada', { content: '<h3>Prince Koresada</h3><p>A son of Emperor Kōkō and an elder brother, by the same mother, of Emperor Uda.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.manajo', { content: '<h3>The Chinese preface (manajo)</h3><p>The <em>Kokin Wakashū</em> has two prefaces: the <em>kanajo</em>, written in Japanese kana and attributed to Ki no Tsurayuki, and the <em>manajo</em>, written in classical Chinese and attributed to Ki no Yoshimochi.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hanafuda', { content: '<h3>Hanafuda (flower cards)</h3><p>Japanese playing cards showing the flowers and plants of the twelve months, four cards for each month.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.basho', { content: '<h3>Matsuo Bashō (1644–1694)</h3><p>A poet of the early Edo period who turned <em>haikai</em> verse from light, comic play into a serious art. He is famous for the travel journal <em>The Narrow Road to the Deep North</em> (<em>Oku no Hosomichi</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.edojidai', { content: '<h3>Edo period (1603–1868)</h3><p>The age when the Tokugawa shoguns ruled Japan from Edo, present-day Tokyo.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.keibutsu', { content: '<h3>Seasonal images (keibutsu)</h3><p>Things that mark the seasons, such as flowers, birds, wind and the moon.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_frost', { content: '<h3>"The Hoar-frost King"</h3><p>Porter\'s poetic image. The original simply says that frost lies white on the bridge the magpies make — that is, the Milky Way.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tanabata', { content: '<h3>Tanabata</h3><p>A legend from China: the Weaver Girl and the Cowherd, lovers parted by the Milky Way, may meet only once a year, on the seventh night of the seventh month, when magpies spread their wings to form a bridge for them. The full story is in the Appendix.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.fujiwarashi', { content: '<h3>The Fujiwara clan</h3><p>A noble family founded by Nakatomi no Kamatari, who was given the name Fujiwara by Emperor Tenji in 669. Its Northern branch came to dominate the court in the Heian period.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.chuunagon', { content: '<h3>Middle Counselor (Chūnagon)</h3><p>A high post in the Council of State (<em>Daijōkan</em>), the highest office of government, ranking just below Upper Counselor (<em>Dainagon</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.onryou', { content: '<h3>Angry spirits (onryō)</h3><p>The spirit of someone who died with a grudge and was believed to bring curses and disasters. Emperor Kanmu had the spirits of Prince Sawara and others enshrined to calm them.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ecchukoku', { content: '<h3>Etchū Province</h3><p>An old province covering present-day Toyama Prefecture.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.inabanokuni', { content: '<h3>Inaba Province</h3><p>An old province covering the eastern part of present-day Tottori Prefecture.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nagaokakyo', { content: '<h3>Nagaoka-kyō</h3><p>The capital from 784 to 794, built by Emperor Kanmu in present-day Mukō, Nagaokakyō and Nishikyō Ward of Kyoto. After ten years the capital moved again, to Heian-kyō.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.daidairi', { content: '<h3>The Greater Palace and the Ōtenmon Gate</h3><img src="/img/daidairi.gif" alt="Plan of the Greater Palace (Daidairi)" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto;">', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hokke', { content: '<h3>The Northern Fujiwara (Fujiwara Hokke)</h3><img src="/img/fujiwara_yonke_diagram.gif?01" alt="The four branches of the Fujiwara" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto;"><p>The branch descended from Fujiwara no Fusasaki. It became the family of regents, and most of the nobles active in the Heian period came from it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yamatonokuni', { content: '<h3>Yamato Province</h3><p>An old province covering present-day Nara Prefecture.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yamatomonogatari', { content: '<h3>Yamato Monogatari</h3><p>A collection of tales built around poems, from the mid-Heian period, with poems and stories of many kinds of people — monks, nobles and the poor.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mikasayama', { content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div><h3>Mount Mikasa</h3><p>A 283 m mountain rising behind Kasuga Taisha Shrine in Nara. Its name is said to come from its cone shape, like an upturned hat (<em>kasa</em>). The <em>Shoku Nihongi</em> records that envoys to Tang China worshipped the gods south of this mountain, praying for a safe journey.</p></div><figure style="margin:0 auto 10px;"><img loading="lazy" src="/img/mikasayama.webp" alt="Mount Mikasa" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto;"><figcaption><small style="color:#fff;">Degueulasse, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons</small></figcaption></figure></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kasuga', { content: '<h3>Kasuga</h3><p>The area of Nara around Kasuga Taisha Shrine.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.morokoshi', { content: '<h3>China (Morokoshi)</h3><p>An old Japanese name for China.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kentoushi', { content: '<h3>Envoys to Tang China (Kentōshi)</h3><p>Official missions that Japan sent to Tang China from 630. They brought back Chinese learning, law and Buddhism. A mission planned in 894 was called off after Sugawara no Michizane advised against it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kakyo', { content: '<h3>Imperial exams (kakyo)</h3><p>The examinations used in old China to choose government officials.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.gensoukoutei', { content: '<h3>Emperor Xuanzong</h3><p>The 9th emperor of Tang China (reigned 712–756). His good rule brought Tang to its peak, but late in life his devotion to Yang Guifei led to the An Lushan Rebellion, which weakened the dynasty.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.choan', { content: '<h3>Chang\'an</h3><p>The capital of the Sui and Tang dynasties, present-day Xi\'an in Shaanxi Province, China.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.rihaku', { content: '<h3>Li Bai (701–762)</h3><p>A poet of the High Tang, counted with Du Fu as one of the greatest poets in Chinese history. For his free, ever-changing style he was later called the "Immortal of Poetry."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.houshi', { content: '<h3>Priest (hōshi)</h3><p>A title for a Buddhist priest who knows the teachings well and guides others.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kakekotoba_waka', { content: '<h3>A pun (kakekotoba)</h3><p>A technique in which one sound carries two meanings at once, widening the poem through word association.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.rokkasen', { content: '<h3>The Six Poetic Sages (Rokkasen)</h3><p>Six poets whom Ki no Tsurayuki picked out and judged in the preface to the <em>Kokin Wakashū</em>: Archbishop Henjō, Ariwara no Narihira, Fun\'ya no Yasuhide, Priest Kisen, Ono no Komachi and Ōtomo no Kuronushi. His judgments were harsh, but the six later came to be praised as great poets.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.genjimonogatari', { content: '<h3>The Tale of Genji</h3><p>A long tale in 54 chapters, written by Murasaki Shikibu in the mid-Heian period (much of it complete by about 1008). It tells of the life, glory and decline of Hikaru Genji.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kanajo', { content: '<h3>The Japanese preface (kanajo)</h3><p>The preface of the <em>Kokin Wakashū</em> written in Japanese kana, by Ki no Tsurayuki.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.otomonokuronushi', { content: '<h3>Ōtomo no Kuronushi</h3><p>An early Heian poet and one of the Six Poetic Sages — the only one with no poem in the Hyakunin Isshu. He came from a family of Ōtomo village in Shiga, Ōmi Province, and is thought to be unrelated to Yakamochi\'s Ōtomo clan.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mumyoushou', { content: '<h3>Mumyōshō</h3><p>A book on poetry by Kamo no Chōmei, written in the Kamakura period.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mimuroto', { content: '<h3>Mimuroto</h3><p>An area of Uji City, Kyoto Prefecture, home of Mimurotoji Temple, famous for its hydrangeas.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.chou', { content: '<h3>Chō (a unit of length)</h3><p>An old unit of length; one <em>chō</em> is about 110 m.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kyouka', { content: '<h3>Kyōka (comic poems)</h3><p>Short poems in the 5-7-5-7-7 form of waka, full of satire, irony and humor.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.bakufu', { content: '<h3>The shogunate (bakufu)</h3><p>The military government led by a shogun. In 1853 this was the Tokugawa shogunate in Edo.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nagame', { content: '<h3>Long rains and gazing (nagame)</h3><p>A <em>kakekotoba</em> (pivot word): <em>nagame</em> means both 長雨, "long rain," and 眺め, "gazing while lost in thought." In the same poem, <em>furu</em> means both "to fall" (of rain) and "to pass" (of time).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.dewa', { content: '<h3>Dewa Province</h3><p>An old province covering present-day Yamagata and Akita Prefectures.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.gunji', { content: '<h3>District official (gunji)</h3><p>An official under the provincial governor who ran a district (<em>gun</em>), the basic unit of local government. Local powerful families were appointed to the post.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sanbi', { content: '<h3>The three great beauties of the world</h3><p>A phrase that became widely known in Japan through the media from the mid-Meiji period (late 19th century). No one knows who first used it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.youkihi', { content: '<h3>Yang Guifei (719–756)</h3><p>The beloved consort of Emperor Xuanzong of Tang China. Because his devotion to her was said to be one cause of the An Lushan Rebellion, she is called a beauty who toppled a country.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ninmyou', { content: '<h3>Emperor Ninmyō</h3><p>The 54th emperor, a son of Emperor Saga.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.montoku', { content: '<h3>Emperor Montoku</h3><p>The 55th emperor, eldest son of Emperor Ninmyō.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sanseki', { content: '<h3>The Three Great Calligraphers (Sanseki)</h3><p>Ono no Michikaze, Fujiwara no Sukemasa and Fujiwara no Yukinari, master calligraphers of the mid-Heian period.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kokugaku', { content: '<h3>Scholars of Japanese classics (kokugaku)</h3><p>A school of study that arose in the mid-Edo period, reading Japan\'s own classics closely. Keichū, Kamo no Mabuchi and Motoori Norinaga are counted as its three great scholars.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sotoorihime', { content: '<div style="display:flex; gap:10px;"><div><h3>Princess Sotoori</h3><p>A legendary beauty who appears in the <em>Kojiki</em> and the <em>Nihon Shoki</em>. Her name, "shining through her robes," comes from her beauty, which was said to glow through her clothes.</p></div><img src="/img/Sotoorihime.gif" alt="Princess Sotoori" style="width:120px; height:auto;"></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.zeami', { content: '<h3>Zeami (c. 1363–c. 1443)</h3><p>A Noh actor and playwright of the early Muromachi period who, with his father Kan\'ami, perfected the art of Noh and wrote many works on it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ousakanoseki', { content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div><h3>The Ōsaka Barrier (Ōsaka no Seki)</h3><p>A checkpoint on the border between Yamashiro Province and Ōmi Province (present-day Kyoto and Shiga). The photo shows the national road where the monument to the Ōsakayama Barrier stands, about 100 m east of Ōtani Station on the Keihan line.</p></div><img loading="lazy" src="/img/ousakayama.webp" alt="The site of the Ōsaka Barrier" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin:0 auto 10px;"></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.anshitsu', { content: '<h3>A hermit\'s hut (anshitsu)</h3><p>A simple dwelling of a monk or a person who has left the world.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tougoku', { content: '<h3>The eastern provinces (Tōgoku)</h3><p>The provinces to the east, as seen from the capital, Kyoto.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.daigo', { content: '<h3>Emperor Daigo</h3><p>The 60th emperor, eldest son of Emperor Uda. He ruled directly without a regent, and his reign was later praised as the "Engi era of good rule." In 905 he had Ki no Tsurayuki and others compile the <em>Kokin Wakashū</em>.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ousakayama', { content: '<h3>Mount Ōsaka</h3><p>A 325 m mountain on the border between Kyoto and Ōtsu. It was an important crossing, and the Ōsaka Barrier stood there.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.biwahoushi', { content: '<h3>Biwa-playing monks (biwa hōshi)</h3><p>Blind monks who made their living playing the biwa (a Japanese lute) in the streets, from the mid-Heian period on.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yamashironokuni', { content: '<h3>Yamashiro Province</h3><p>An old province covering the southern part of present-day Kyoto Prefecture.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ouminokuni', { content: '<h3>Ōmi Province</h3><p>An old province covering present-day Shiga Prefecture.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.toukaidou', { content: '<h3>Tōkaidō</h3><p>The great highway along the Pacific coast of central Honshu. In the Edo period it ran from Nihonbashi in Edo to Sanjō Ōhashi in Kyoto, with 53 post towns on the way.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.michinaga', { content: '<h3>Fujiwara no Michinaga</h3><p>A powerful noble of the mid-Heian period, fifth son of Fujiwara no Kaneie. As grandfather of three emperors (Go-Ichijō, Go-Suzaku and Go-Reizei) through his daughters, he led the government.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nenohi', { content: '<h3>Hatsune no Hi</h3><p>The first Day of the Rat of a month, especially of the New Year. In old times the court held banquets on this day, and people went out to the fields to pull up young pines and pick spring greens.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yasoshima', { content: '<h3>Many islands (yasoshima)</h3><p>Literally "eighty islands": a great many islands.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hairu', { content: '<h3>Exile (hairu)</h3><p>Being banished, usually to a far-off island, as a punishment.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.oki', { content: '<h3>Oki Province</h3><p>An old province made up of the islands north of present-day Shimane Prefecture. In the Heian period, Oki was known as a place of exile.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kentoufukushi', { content: '<h3>Deputy envoy (kentō fukushi)</h3><p>The second-ranking member of a mission to Tang China, next below the ambassador.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kanshi_poem', { content: '<h3>A Chinese poem (kanshi)</h3><p>Poems written in classical Chinese. Heian courtiers wrote them as a mark of their learning.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.saga', { content: '<h3>Emperor Saga</h3><p>The 52nd emperor, said to have been the fifth son of Emperor Kanmu (there are other views).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.onsha', { content: '<h3>Pardon (onsha)</h3><p>A reduction or cancelling of a punishment by order of the emperor.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.daigakuryou', { content: '<h3>The court university (Daigakuryō)</h3><p>The school under the <em>ritsuryō</em> system that trained government officials. Students of literature (<em>monjōshō</em>) studied Chinese literature and history there.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ryounogige', { content: '<h3>Ryō no Gige</h3><p>An official commentary on the Yōrō Code, completed in 833 by order of Emperor Junna. It had the force of law. Kiyohara no Natsuno led the work, and Ono no Takamura was one of its compilers.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sangi', { content: '<h3>Councillor (Sangi)</h3><p>A post in the Council of State (<em>Daijōkan</em>) that was added outside the original law codes, ranking next below the counselors (<em>nagon</em>). The name means "taking part in deliberations" on government.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kugyou', { content: '<h3>Kugyō (top nobles)</h3><p>The highest officials of the court: the ministers, the counselors, Councillors, and others of Third Rank and above.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.dazaifu', { content: '<h3>Dazaifu</h3><p>A government office in Chikuzen Province (present-day Fukuoka Prefecture) that ran Kyushu and was in charge of defense and foreign relations.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hakkyoi', { content: '<h3>Bai Juyi (772–846)</h3><p>A Tang Chinese poet, also called Bai Letian. His collected works reached Japan in the 9th century and were deeply loved by Heian writers such as Murasaki Shikibu and Sei Shōnagon.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.goudanshou', { content: '<h3>Gōdanshō</h3><p>A collection of talks by the scholar Ōe no Masafusa on Chinese poetry, court customs, music and more, written down by Fujiwara no Sanekane around 1104–1108.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ujishuuimonogatari', { content: '<h3>Uji Shūi Monogatari</h3><p>A collection of tales thought to date from the early Kamakura period, with many Buddhist stories and characters from nobles to common people.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.konjaku', { content: '<h3>Konjaku Monogatari Shū</h3><p>A large collection of tales from the late Heian period, in three parts: India, China and Japan. Each tale begins "<em>Ima wa mukashi</em>" ("Long ago…").</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.enma', { content: '<h3>King Enma</h3><p>In Buddhism, the king of the underworld who judges the dead.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_angels12', { content: '<h3>"Angel wings"</h3><p>Porter\'s free rendering. The original speaks of <em>otome</em>, "maidens" — the Gosechi dancers, seen as heavenly maidens (<em>tennyo</em>) come down to earth. There are no angels in the poem.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.zokumyou', { content: '<h3>Lay name (Zokumyō)</h3><p>The name a person used before becoming a Buddhist monk.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kuroudonotou', { content: '<h3>Head Chamberlain (Kurōdo no Tō)</h3><p>The head of the <em>kurōdo</em>, the emperor\'s private secretaries.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ryouan', { content: '<h3>Mourning period (ryōan)</h3><p>The period during which the emperor mourned the death of a parent; here, the mourning for Emperor Ninmyō.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.soujou', { content: '<h3>Sōjō (High Priest)</h3><p>The highest post among the officials who supervised Buddhist monks.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shinnousenge', { content: '<h3>Imperial prince (shinnō)</h3><p>Being born into the imperial line did not make someone an imperial prince (<em>shinnō</em>) automatically. From the Heian period, the title was granted only by an order of the emperor (<em>shinnō senge</em>), and Yasuyo was never given it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shinsekikouka', { content: '<h3>Leaving the imperial family (shinseki kōka)</h3><p>When a member of the imperial family is given a family name and becomes a subject. In the early Heian period so many royal children were born that their allowances strained the state\'s finances, so those with no chance of the throne were often made subjects. Emperor Saga gave three of his sons the name Minamoto in 814, the start of the Minamoto clan.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.dainagon', { content: '<h3>Upper Counselor (Dainagon)</h3><p>A high post in the Council of State (<em>Daijōkan</em>), next below the Minister of the Right.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tendaishu', { content: '<h3>Tendai school</h3><p>A school of Buddhism founded in Japan by Saichō, with its head temple, Enryaku-ji, on Mount Hiei.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.toyoakarinosechie', { content: '<h3>Toyo-no-Akari no Sechie</h3><p>A court banquet held after the Daijōsai and the Niinamesai harvest festivals.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.daijousai', { content: '<h3>Daijōsai (the enthronement harvest rite)</h3><p>A Shinto rite held once, when a new emperor takes the throne, in which the emperor prays for good harvests and for the peace of the nation and its people.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.niinamesai', { content: '<h3>Niinamesai</h3><p>A yearly Shinto rite at court in which the emperor prays for good harvests and for the peace of the nation and its people.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.junihitoe', { content: '<h3>Jūni-hitoe</h3><p>The formal dress of court ladies and noblewomen: many layered robes. One theory says the name means "worn in full twelve-fold."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hiougi', { content: '<h3>Cypress-wood fans (hiōgi)</h3><p>A folding fan made of thin slats of Japanese cypress, used at court. In the Heian period, commoners are said to have been forbidden to use it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hitaigushi', { content: '<h3>Ornamental combs (hitaigushi)</h3><p>An ornamental comb that women wore above the forehead with formal dress.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shinchokusen', { content: '<h3>Shin Chokusen Wakashū</h3><p>The ninth imperial anthology of waka, completed in 1235 by order of Emperor Go-Horikawa. Fujiwara no Sadaie (Teika) began it alone. It has a little over 1,370 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.issa', { content: '<h3>Kobayashi Issa (1763–1828)</h3><p>A haiku poet of the late Edo period, born in Kashiwabara, Shinano Province (present-day Nagano Prefecture). His real name was Kobayashi Yatarō.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.haigaika', { content: '<h3>Haikaika</h3><p>Waka with a humorous touch in their wording or content.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jokotoba13', { content: '<h3>"My love has gathered deep": a preface (jokotoba)</h3><p>The first half of the poem, <em>Tsukubane no / mine yori otsuru / Minanogawa</em>, is a <em>jokotoba</em>: a scene from nature that leads into the poet\'s feelings. Like the river gathering into deep pools, his love has piled up deep inside him.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tsukubane', { content: '<h3>Mount Tsukuba</h3><p>An 877 m mountain in present-day Ibaraki Prefecture. It has two peaks, the "Male Peak" and the "Female Peak," and was a place where young men and women gathered to sing love songs to each other (<em>utagaki</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tsuridono', { content: '<h3>The Princess of Tsuridono</h3><p>Princess Suishi was called this because she lived in the Tsuridono-in, the residence of her father, Emperor Kōkō.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.koukou', { content: '<h3>Emperor Kōkō</h3><p>The 58th emperor, third son of Emperor Ninmyō. He took the throne on the recommendation of Fujiwara no Mototsune.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mototsune', { content: '<h3>Fujiwara no Mototsune</h3><p>A powerful noble of the early Heian period. He was regent for Yōzei and played a part in putting Emperors Kōkō and Uda on the throne. An edict of Emperor Uda told officials to consult (<em>azukari mōsu</em>) Mototsune first on all matters — the first appearance of the word <em>kanpaku</em> — so he is known as the first Kanpaku.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.joukou', { content: '<h3>Retired emperor (Jōkō)</h3><p>An emperor who has stepped down from the throne.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kanpaku', { content: '<h3>Kampaku (chief adviser)</h3><p>The post of assisting an adult emperor in government, ranking above the Grand Minister.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sesshou', { content: '<h3>Regent (sesshō)</h3><p>The post of ruling on behalf of an emperor who is still a child, ranking above the Grand Minister.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.genpuku', { content: '<h3>Coming-of-age ceremony (genpuku)</h3><p>The ceremony marking a boy\'s coming of age.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.seiwa', { content: '<h3>Emperor Seiwa</h3><p>The 56th emperor, Retired Emperor Yōzei\'s father.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.uda', { content: '<h3>Emperor Uda</h3><p>The 59th emperor, seventh son of Emperor Kōkō.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mutsu', { content: '<h3>Mutsu (Michinoku)</h3><p>An old province in the far northeast of Honshu, covering present-day Fukushima, Miyagi, Iwate and Aomori Prefectures and part of Akita. It was also called <em>Michinoku</em>, "the back of the road."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shiogama', { content: '<h3>Shiogama</h3><p>A place in central Miyagi Prefecture, known for making salt by boiling down seawater.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sadaijin', { content: '<h3>Minister of the Left (Sadaijin)</h3><p>The head of the Council of State (<em>Daijōkan</em>), the highest office of government — in practice, the top of the government.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ookagami', { content: '<h3>Ōkagami</h3><p>A historical tale of the late Heian period by an unknown author. It covers 176 years of court history, from 850 to 1025, centered on the glory of the Northern Fujiwara, especially Fujiwara no Michinaga.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.isemonogatari', { content: '<h3>The Tales of Ise</h3><p>A Heian-period collection of tales built around poems. Its hero is a man who recalls Ariwara no Narihira.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kariginu', { content: '<h3>Kariginu</h3><p>A robe that nobles wore for hunting. From the mid-Heian period it became everyday wear.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kasugano', { content: '<h3>Kasuga Field (Kasugano)</h3><p>The fields at the western foot of Mount Kasuga, a favorite outing spot of Heian nobles — around present-day Nara Park.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_mother', { content: '<h3>"Mother, for thy sake"</h3><p>Porter\'s addition. The original only says <em>kimi ga tame</em>, "for you," and does not say who received the greens.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.wakana', { content: '<h3>Young greens (wakana)</h3><p>Edible plants that have just sprouted in spring. At court they were made into a hot soup and eaten on the first Day of the Rat of the New Year, in the belief that they kept all illness away.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ninna', { content: '<h3>Emperor Kōkō (the Ninna Emperor)</h3><p>The headnote in the <em>Kokin Wakashū</em> calls him "the Ninna Emperor," after the era name of his reign. He was also called "the Komatsu Emperor," after his tomb.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sandaijitesuroku', { content: '<h3>A history of the time: the Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku</h3><p>An official history compiled in the Heian period. It records, year by year, the 30 years of the reigns of Emperors Seiwa, Yōzei and Kōkō (858–887).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kakekotoba16', { content: '<h3>"Waiting for me": two pivot words (kakekotoba)</h3><p><em>Inaba</em> means both 因幡, the province, and 往なば, "if I go." <em>Matsu</em> means both 松, "pine," and 待つ, "to wait." The pines on the mountain become the people waiting for him.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.inabanokami', { content: '<h3>Governor of Inaba (Inaba no Kami)</h3><p>Inaba Province covered the eastern part of present-day Tottori Prefecture. The <em>kami</em> was the head of a provincial government, something like a prefectural governor today.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.inabayama', { content: '<h3>Mount Inaba</h3><p>A gentle 249 m mountain in the east of Tottori City, near the site of the Inaba provincial office.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.heizei', { content: '<h3>Emperor Heizei</h3><p>The 51st emperor, said to have been a son of Emperor Kanmu (there are different views).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shougakuin', { content: '<h3>Shōgakuin</h3><p>A school founded by Ariwara no Yukihira for his clan. Young men of the imperial family and of clans that had left it (the Minamoto, Taira and Ariwara) lodged there while studying at the court university (<em>Daigakuryō</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kusukonohen', { content: '<h3>The Kusuko Incident (810)</h3><p>A clash between Retired Emperor Heizei and his younger brother, Emperor Saga. Emperor Saga moved troops quickly and Heizei became a monk; Fujiwara no Kusuko, who served Heizei, and her elder brother Nakanari were punished.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.dazainogonnosochi', { content: '<h3>Acting Governor-General of Dazaifu (Dazai no Gon no Sotsu)</h3><p>The deputy head of Dazaifu in Kyushu. The top post was customarily given to an imperial prince, so the deputy was in practice the head.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.suma', { content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div><h3>Suma</h3><p>A stretch of coast in the west of Kobe, Hyogo Prefecture. Its name is said to come from <em>sumi</em>, "corner," because it lay at the western corner of the capital region.</p></div><img loading="lazy" src="/img/suma.webp" alt="Suma" style="display:block; width:250px; height:auto; margin:0 auto 10px;"></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_song', { content: '<h3>"So sweet a song"</h3><p>Porter\'s free rendering. The original has no song: it says the river "tie-dyes its water in deep Chinese crimson" (<em>karakurenai ni mizu kukuru</em>), the red leaves on the water looking like a dyed pattern.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.chihayaburu', { content: '<h3>The pillow word <em>chihayaburu</em></h3><p>In the original, <em>kami</em> (gods) is preceded by <em>chihayaburu</em>, a pillow word (<em>makurakotoba</em>) that leads into it. It means mighty, fierce, of overwhelming power.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tatsutagawa', { content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div><h3>The Tatsuta River</h3><p>A river in Ikaruga, Nara Prefecture, flowing past the foot of Mount Mimuro. It was famous in poetry for its autumn leaves.</p></div><img loading="lazy" src="/img/tatsutagawa.webp" alt="The Tatsuta River" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin:0 auto 10px;"></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.karakurenai', { content: '<h3>Deep crimson (karakurenai)</h3><p>"Chinese crimson": a vivid red, as of dye brought from abroad.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.byoubuuta', { content: '<h3>Screen poems (byōbu-uta)</h3><p>Poems written on the theme of a picture painted on a folding screen. The custom came from China, where Chinese poems were added to screen paintings; in Japan, waka came to be added to Japanese-style paintings.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.takaiko', { content: '<h3>Fujiwara no Takaiko (the Nijō Empress)</h3><p>A consort (<em>nyōgo</em>) of Emperor Seiwa, younger sister of Fujiwara no Mototsune, and mother of Emperor Yōzei. She was called the Nijō Empress because she lived at the Nijō-in after Yōzei stepped down.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.saigu', { content: '<h3>Saigū (the Ise Priestess)</h3><p>An unmarried imperial princess who served at the Ise Shrine.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.oriku', { content: '<h3>Oriku (acrostic)</h3><p>A technique of placing the sounds of a word, one by one, at the start of each line of a poem.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.koretakashinnou', { content: '<h3>Prince Koretaka</h3><p>The first son of Emperor Montoku, the 55th emperor.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ason', { content: '<h3>Ason</h3><p>An honorific title used from the Heian period for people of the Fifth Rank and above. For the Third Rank and above it was placed after the family name; for the Fourth and Fifth Ranks, after the personal name.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.suminoe', { content: '<h3>Suminoe</h3><p>Present-day Sumiyoshi Ward, Osaka. Land has since been reclaimed, but the sea once came close to Sumiyoshi Taisha Shrine, and a tall stone lantern there guided ships into port.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hokekyou', { content: '<h3>The Lotus Sutra (Hokekyō)</h3><p>One of the most important scriptures of Mahayana Buddhism. It had reached Japan by the time of Prince Shōtoku in the 6th century and was honored as a sutra that protects the nation.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kuroudo', { content: '<h3>Kurōdo (Chamberlain)</h3><p>The emperor\'s private secretaries, who passed on his orders and handled his affairs.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nanke', { content: '<h3>The Southern House of the Fujiwara (Nanke)</h3><img src="/img/fujiwara_yonke_diagram.gif?01" alt="The four branches of the Fujiwara" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto;"><p>One of the four branches of the Fujiwara, founded by Fujiwara no Muchimaro.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sumiyoshitaisha', { content: '<h3>Sumiyoshi Taisha</h3><p>A shrine in Sumiyoshi Ward, Osaka, the head shrine of all Sumiyoshi shrines in Japan. Its gods were worshipped in ancient times as gods of voyages and harbors, and from the Heian period also as gods of waka poetry.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_19', { content: '<h3>"I hope the time may be…"</h3><p>Porter turns the poem into a wish that their time apart will be short. The original is bitter: must she spend her whole life without meeting him even for a moment?</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.fushinoma', { content: '<h3>A joint of reed (fushi no ma)</h3><p>"The space between two joints of a reed," used as an image of something very short. It also means "a brief moment," so the phrase carries both meanings (a <em>kakekotoba</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.naniwagata', { content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div><h3>Naniwa Bay (Naniwagata)</h3><p>An inlet of Osaka Bay where reeds grew thickly; a famous place in poetry (<em>utamakura</em>).</p></div><img loading="lazy" src="/img/naniwagata.webp?20240421" alt="Naniwa Bay" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin:0 auto 10px;"></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kashuu', { content: '<h3>Ise Shū: a personal poetry collection (kashū)</h3><p>The <em>Ise Shū</em> is Ise\'s <em>kashū</em>: a collection of one poet\'s own poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.miyasudokoro', { content: '<h3>Miyasudokoro</h3><p>Originally the emperor\'s resting room; later, a title for a woman who served the emperor in his private chambers.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.houou', { content: '<h3>Cloistered emperor (Hōō)</h3><p>A retired emperor who has become a Buddhist monk. The title <em>Daijō Hōō</em> was first used when Retired Emperor Uda became a monk.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.miotsukushi20', { content: '<h3>"Sacrifice everything": a pivot word (miotsukushi)</h3><p>In the original, <em>mi o tsukushite mo</em>, "even if I give up my whole self," is also heard as <em>miotsukushi</em>, the channel markers of Naniwa Bay (see the Appendix). The poem places this word right after "Naniwa."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jkyogokunomiyasudokoro', { content: '<h3>The Kyōgoku consort (Kyōgoku no Miyasudokoro)</h3><p>Fujiwara no Hōshi, a consort (<em>nyōgo</em>) of Retired Emperor Uda.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.youzei', { content: '<h3>Emperor Yōzei</h3><p>The 57th emperor. Prince Motoyoshi was his son by Fujiwara no Takaiko.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nagatsuki', { content: '<h3>The long autumn month (Nagatsuki)</h3><p>The ninth month of the old calendar, roughly late September to early November today.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.innreki', { content: '<h3>Lunar month (the old calendar)</h3><p>Japan\'s old calendar followed the waxing and waning of the moon, adding an extra month every few years to keep in step with the seasons.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ariake', { content: '<h3>The ariake moon (ariake no tsuki)</h3><p>The moon still in the sky at dawn, seen after about the 16th day of each lunar month.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.utakai', { content: '<h3>Poetry gatherings (utakai)</h3><p>Meetings where several poets wrote waka on the same set theme.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.urinin', { content: '<h3>Urin-in Temple</h3><p>A temple in Murasakino, Kita Ward, Kyoto. It began as a country palace of Emperor Junna, passed to Emperor Ninmyō\'s son Prince Tsuneyasu, and after the prince\'s death in 869 was entrusted to Henjō and made a temple.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yoshifusa', { content: '<h3>Fujiwara no Yoshifusa (804–872)</h3><p>A leading noble of the early Heian period, the first person outside the imperial family to become regent (<em>sesshō</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kourui', { content: '<h3>Crimson tears (kōrui)</h3><p>In waka, tears of the deepest grief, imagined as red as blood.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_22', { content: '<h3>"Hurricane… hurries canes"</h3><p>Porter imitates the poem\'s wordplay with an English pun. The original plays with Chinese characters instead: 山 (mountain) + 風 (wind) = 嵐 (<em>arashi</em>, storm).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.rigoushi', { content: '<h3>A word game with characters (rigōshi)</h3><p>A technique of splitting a Chinese character into its parts, or joining parts into one character, and weaving them into a poem.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.arashi', { content: '<h3>Arashi</h3><p>In old Japanese, a strong wind blowing down from the mountains.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mikawa', { content: '<h3>Mikawa Province</h3><p>An old province covering the eastern part of present-day Aichi Prefecture.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nuidononosuke', { content: '<h3>Assistant Head of the Palace Sewing Office (Nuidono no Suke)</h3><p>The second-ranking official of the office, under the Ministry of Central Affairs, that oversaw court ladies and the sewing of court clothing.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yakusanokabane', { content: '<h3>The Eight Titles (Yakusa no Kabane)</h3><p>Eight hereditary titles for clans set up by Emperor Tenmu in 684. From the top: <em>mahito</em>, <em>ason</em>, <em>sukune</em>, <em>imiki</em>, <em>michinoshi</em>, <em>omi</em>, <em>muraji</em> and <em>inagi</em>.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.senzaishu', { content: '<h3>Senzai Wakashū</h3><p>The seventh imperial anthology of waka, completed in 1187 by order of Retired Emperor Go-Shirakawa and compiled by Fujiwara no Toshinari. It contains 1,288 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.taiku23', { content: '<h3>"A thousand" and "one": a Chinese technique</h3><p>Chinese poetry often sets two contrasting words side by side (<em>taiku</em>, parallelism). Here <em>chiji ni</em>, "in a thousand ways," is balanced against <em>waga mi hitotsu</em>, "I alone."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kudaiwaka', { content: '<h3>Kudai Waka</h3><p>Waka written on lines taken from Chinese poems. Chisato presented his collection to Emperor Uda in 897.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hyobudaijou', { content: '<h3>A senior secretary in the Ministry of Military Affairs (Hyōbu no Daijō)</h3><p>A third-ranking official of the ministry in charge of military affairs and defense.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hakushimonju', { content: '<h3>Collected Works of Bai Juyi (Hakushi Monjū)</h3><p>The collected poems and writings of the Tang poet Bai Juyi. It reached Japan in the 9th century and was loved by Heian readers; Murasaki Shikibu and Sei Shōnagon both drew on it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.oborodukiyo', { content: '<h3>Oborozukiyo ("hazy moonlit night")</h3><p>A spring night when the moon is softly blurred by mist or high clouds. It is also the name of a graceful, high-born lady in <em>The Tale of Genji</em>.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.houjouki', { content: '<h3>An Account of My Hut (Hōjōki)</h3><p>An essay by Kamo no Chōmei from the Kamakura period. It famously opens: "The flowing river never stops, and yet the water is never the same water…"</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.wakanroueishu', { content: '<h3>Wakan Rōeishū</h3><p>A collection compiled by Fujiwara no Kintō of 216 waka and 588 lines of Chinese poetry suited to being chanted aloud.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.monjohakase', { content: '<h3>Professor of Literature (monjō hakase)</h3><p>A teacher at the court university (<em>Daigakuryō</em>) in the department of history and Chinese literature — the highest post for a scholar.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kakekotoba24', { content: '<h3>"This time" and "this journey" (kakekotoba)</h3><p><em>Kono tabi</em> means both "this time" (此の度) and "this journey" (この旅). <em>Tamuke</em>, too, is both an offering to the gods of the road and the name of the mountain.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nusa', { content: '<div><h3>Nusa (offerings)</h3><div style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:10px;"><img src="/img/nusa.webp" alt="Nusa" style="width:100px; height:auto;"><p>Strips of paper or hemp offered to the gods when praying for a safe journey.</p></div></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tamukeyama', { content: '<h3>Mount Tamuke</h3><p>A mountain where travelers made offerings (<em>tamuke</em>) to the god of the road. It is also said to be the Mount Tamuke in eastern Nara, at the foot of which stands Tamukeyama Hachiman Shrine.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nishiki', { content: '<h3>Brocade (nishiki)</h3><p>A thick silk woven with gold and silver threads and many colors into beautiful patterns.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.udaijin', { content: '<h3>Minister of the Right (Udaijin)</h3><p>The second-highest post in the Council of State (<em>Daijōkan</em>), under the Minister of the Left — the number two of the government.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shoutainohen', { content: '<h3>The Shōtai Incident (901)</h3><p>Fujiwara no Tokihira falsely told Emperor Daigo that Michizane planned to put his son-in-law, Prince Tokiyo, on the throne, and Michizane was sent away to Dazaifu.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sanekazura', { content: '<h3>The vine (sanekazura)</h3><p>An evergreen climbing vine with red berries. In the original, its name also contains <em>sa-ne</em>, "to sleep together."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.gaiseki', { content: '<h3>A relative of the emperor by marriage (gaiseki)</h3><p>A relative on the emperor\'s mother\'s side. Sadakata\'s sister was Emperor Daigo\'s mother.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ogurayama', { content: '<h3>Mount Ogura</h3><p>A mountain in Saga, Kyoto, facing Arashiyama across the Ōi River. Fujiwara no Sadaie is thought to have compiled the Hyakunin Isshu at his villa there.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shigou', { content: '<h3>A name given after death (shigō)</h3><p>A name granted to a person of high rank after death. "Teishin-kō" is Tadahira\'s.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.engishiki', { content: '<h3>Engishiki</h3><p>A collection of detailed rules for carrying out the law codes, in 50 volumes, completed in 927 and put into effect in 967. Salaries for each rank and post were also based on it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jouheitengyou', { content: '<h3>The revolts of Masakado and Sumitomo (Tengyō no Ran)</h3><p>Two revolts of the mid-Heian period, 935–941: Taira no Masakado\'s in the Kantō region and Fujiwara no Sumitomo\'s in the Seto Inland Sea.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.fukakusa', { content: '<h3>Fukakusa</h3><p>An area of Fushimi Ward, Kyoto.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shuigusou', { content: '<h3>Shūi Gusō</h3><p>Fujiwara no Sadaie\'s personal collection of his own poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shikanokomoyou', { content: '<h3>Fawn pattern (kanoko moyō)</h3><p>The white spots on the light brown back of a young deer.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_27', { content: '<h3>"The maid I saw but now"</h3><p>Porter says the poet has just seen her. The original says the opposite: he asks "when did I ever see you?" — he loves someone he has hardly or never seen.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jokotoba27', { content: '<h3>"Like the Izumi River": a preface (jokotoba)</h3><p>The first half, <em>Mika no hara / wakite nagaruru / Izumigawa</em>, is a <em>jokotoba</em>: the river\'s name, <em>Izumi</em>, leads by sound into <em>itsu mi</em>, "when did I see."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mikanohara', { content: '<h3>Mika Plain (Mikanohara)</h3><p>The north bank of the Kizu River in present-day Kamo, Kizugawa City, Kyoto Prefecture. Emperor Shōmu moved the capital here from Heijō-kyō in 740 and built Kuni-kyō.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kaimami', { content: '<h3>A glimpse (kaimami)</h3><p>Peeking at someone secretly through a gap. High-born Heian women lived hidden from others\' eyes, so men glimpsing them through fences or blinds often appear in classical tales.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.koui', { content: '<h3>Kōi (a lower-ranking consort)</h3><p>A consort of the emperor ranking below a <em>nyōgo</em>.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kuninomiya', { content: '<h3>Kuni-kyō</h3><p>A capital that Emperor Shōmu ordered moved here from Heijō-kyō in 740. It was never finished; work stopped at the end of 743, and in 748 its Great Hall was given to the Yamashiro provincial temple.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kokubunji', { content: '<h3>Provincial temples (kokubunji)</h3><p>Temples that Emperor Shōmu ordered built in every province in 741, so that Buddhism would protect the nation.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kakekotoba28', { content: '<h3>"Disappear" and "wither": a pivot word (kakekotoba)</h3><p><em>Karenu</em> means both 離れぬ, "people stop coming," and 枯れぬ, "the grass withers."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kokoroate', { content: '<h3>Kokoro ate ni</h3><p>"By guessing" — or "with care, intending to." The poem can be read either way.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ishiyamadera', { content: '<h3>Ishiyama-dera Temple</h3><p>A temple of the Shingon school in Ōtsu, Shiga Prefecture. A legend says Murasaki Shikibu got the idea for <em>The Tale of Genji</em> while staying there, though her own diary and poems do not mention it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.chouyounosekku', { content: '<h3>The Chrysanthemum Festival (Chōyō no Sekku)</h3><p>One of the five seasonal festivals, on the ninth day of the ninth month. In China odd numbers were thought lucky, and nine, the highest, doubled made this day special. It reached Japan in the early Heian period, and chrysanthemums were displayed to ward off evil and pray for long life.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.masaokashiki', { content: '<h3>Masaoka Shiki (1867–1902)</h3><p>A haiku and tanka poet of the Meiji period who reformed both forms in his short life, calling for poems that sketch real life (<em>shasei</em>). His <em>Letters to a Waka Poet</em> rejected the <em>Kokin Wakashū</em> and praised the <em>Man\'yōshū</em>.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mitate', { content: '<h3>Mitate</h3><p>A technique of seeing one thing as something else — here, white chrysanthemums as frost, or frost as chrysanthemums.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ariake30', { content: '<h3>The cold dawn moon (ariake)</h3><p>In the original, it is the <em>ariake</em> moon — the moon still in the sky at dawn — that looked cold and heartless (<em>tsurenaku</em>) as they parted.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tentokudairinoutaawase', { content: '<h3>The Poetry Contest of the Imperial Palace (Tentoku Dairi Utaawase)</h3><p>A poetry contest held by Emperor Murakami at the Seiryōden in 960. It became the model for later poetry contests.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.asaborake', { content: '<h3>Asaborake</h3><p>The time when night begins to grow faintly light.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yoshino', { content: '<h3>Yoshino</h3><p>The area around Mount Yoshino in present-day Yoshino Town, Nara Prefecture. In the Heian period it was famous for snow in winter and cherry blossoms in spring.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tamuramaro', { content: '<h3>Sakanoue no Tamuramaro</h3><p>A general of the early Heian period who, as <em>Seii Taishōgun</em> (shogun), subdued the Emishi.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.emishi', { content: '<h3>The Emishi</h3><p>A name for the people (mostly in the northeast) who resisted the rule of the court\'s <em>ritsuryō</em> state.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kemari', { content: '<h3>Kemari (court football)</h3><p>A noble pastime of keeping a ball in the air by kicking it. There was no winner or loser: what mattered was passing the ball so the next player could kick it easily.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kokushi', { content: '<h3>Provincial official (kokushi)</h3><p>An official sent by the court to govern a province. There were four ranks: governor (<em>kami</em>), assistant governor (<em>suke</em>), secretary (<em>jō</em>) and clerk (<em>sakan</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yamatonogonnoshoujou', { content: '<h3>Acting Junior Secretary of Yamato (Yamato no Gon no Shōjō)</h3><p>A third-ranking provincial official of Yamato. Yamato was a top-rank province, so its secretary post was split into senior and junior; "acting" (<em>gon</em>) means appointed beyond the fixed number.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shigarami', { content: '<h3>A weir (shigarami)</h3><p>Stakes driven into a river with bamboo or branches woven between them to hold back the flow.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shiganoyamagoe', { content: '<h3>The Shiga pass (Shiga no Yamagoe)</h3><p>A mountain road from Kyoto over Mount Uryū to present-day Ōtsu. It was known as the approach to Sūfuku-ji (Shiga Temple), founded by Emperor Tenji; the temple was abandoned in the Heian period, but huge foundation stones remain.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mononobe', { content: '<h3>The Mononobe clan</h3><p>A powerful ancient clan that grew into a leading military family, alongside the Ōtomo.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.monjoushou', { content: '<h3>Student of literature (monjōshō)</h3><p>A student of history and Chinese literature at the court university (<em>Daigakuryō</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ikinokami', { content: '<h3>Governor of Iki (Iki no Kami)</h3><p>Iki Province is present-day Iki City, Nagasaki Prefecture, an island. The <em>kami</em> was the head of a provincial government, something like a prefectural governor.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.takasago', { content: '<div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px;"><div><h3>Takasago</h3><p>Present-day Takasago City, Hyōgo Prefecture, on the delta where the Kako River flows into the Seto Inland Sea — long famous for its pines. The photo shows the "Aioi pine" at Takasago Shrine, a symbol of marriage, harmony and long life.</p></div><figure style="margin:0 auto 10px;"><img loading="lazy" src="/img/takasago-no-matsu.webp?02" alt="The Aioi pine at Takasago Shrine" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto;"><figcaption><small>弥, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons</small></figcaption></figure></div>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.rokyo', { content: '<h3>Old age (rōkyō)</h3><p>People in the Heian period lived much shorter lives, so 40 was already counted as the start of old age. Nobles even held a "fortieth-year celebration" (<em>yoso no ga</em>) to pray for long life.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kyouke', { content: '<h3>The Capital House of the Fujiwara (Kyōke)</h3><img src="/img/fujiwara_yonke_diagram.gif?01" alt="The four branches of the Fujiwara" style="display:block; width:100%; height:auto;"><p>One of the four branches of the Fujiwara, founded by Fujiwara no Maro.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sakyoudaibu', { content: '<h3>Master of the Left Capital (Sakyō no Daibu)</h3><p>The head of the office that ran the justice, police and civil affairs of the left (eastern) half of the capital.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kanpyo', { content: '<h3>Kanpyō era (889–898)</h3><p>One of Japan\'s era names, used in the reigns of Emperors Uda and Daigo.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_35', { content: '<h3>"The village of my youth"</h3><p>Porter makes it his childhood home. In the original, <em>furusato</em> means a familiar place he used to visit — the inn at Hase — and the first line, <em>hito wa isa kokoro mo shirazu</em>, says "I cannot know your heart," a teasing reply to the innkeeper.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hatsuse', { content: '<h3>Hase (Hatsuse)</h3><p>A place in Sakurai, Nara Prefecture, known for Hase-dera Temple. From the mid-Heian period its Kannon drew many pilgrims from the nobility.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tosanikki', { content: '<h3>The Tosa Diary (Tosa Nikki)</h3><p>A diary written in kana by Ki no Tsurayuki about his 55-day journey home to the capital after five years as governor of Tosa. He wrote it as if a woman were the author.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mokunogonnnokami', { content: '<h3>Acting Head of the Carpentry Office (Moku no Gon no Kami)</h3><p>The head of the office under the Ministry of the Imperial Household that built palace halls and cut timber. "Acting" (<em>gon</em>) means appointed beyond the fixed number.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.takeuchinosukune', { content: '<h3>Takeuchi no Sukune</h3><p>A legendary statesman of the early Yamato court, said to be the ancestor of central clans such as the Soga, Heguri, Ki and Katsuragi. He is said to have served five emperors and lived over 300 years.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shinsenwaka', { content: '<h3>Shinsen Waka</h3><p>A selection of the finest poems that Emperor Daigo ordered Tsurayuki to make just before he left to govern Tosa. He finished it in Tosa, but Daigo had died by the time he returned, so it was never presented.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ichijou', { content: '<h3>Emperor Ichijō</h3><p>The 66th emperor, first son of Emperor En\'yū. His mother was Fujiwara no Senshi and his grandfather Fujiwara no Kaneie.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.teishi', { content: '<h3>Fujiwara no Teishi</h3><p>Empress of Emperor Ichijō and eldest daughter of the Kampaku Fujiwara no Michitaka. Sei Shōnagon served her.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.engi', { content: '<h3>Engi era (901–923)</h3><p>One of Japan\'s era names, used in the reign of Emperor Daigo.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kanpyouutaawase', { content: '<h3>The Poetry Contest at the Palace of the Empress in the Kanpyō Era</h3><p>A poetry contest held around 889 at the home of Princess Hanshi, mother of Emperor Uda: 100 rounds on the four seasons and love.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.daizennoshoushin', { content: '<h3>A junior official of the Office of the Palace Kitchens (Daizen no Shōjō)</h3><p>A third-ranking official of the office under the Ministry of the Imperial Household that prepared meals and banquets for the court and its ceremonies.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.onshi', { content: '<h3>Fujiwara no Onshi (885–954)</h3><p>Empress of Emperor Daigo and daughter of the Kampaku Fujiwara no Mototsune; mother of Emperors Suzaku and Murakami.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nyoubou', { content: '<h3>Lady-in-waiting (nyōbō)</h3><p>A woman who served a person of high rank at court or in a noble house, looking after them and teaching them. She was given her own room (<em>bō</em>), partitioned by screens, and lived in.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ukonenoshoushou', { content: '<h3>Minor Captain of the Right Palace Guards (Ukon\'e no Shōshō)</h3><p>A deputy commander of the Right Palace Guards, next below the Middle Captain. The palace guards kept watch inside the palace and attended the emperor on his journeys.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shikashu', { content: '<h3>Shika Wakashū</h3><p>The sixth imperial anthology of waka, completed in 1151 by order of Retired Emperor Sutoku and compiled by Fujiwara no Akisuke. It contains 415 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jokotoba39', { content: '<h3>"Though I try to hide it": a preface (jokotoba)</h3><p>The original opens with <em>Asajiu no / ono no shinohara</em>, "the low bamboo grass (<em>shino</em>) of the moor," which leads by sound into <em>shinoburedo</em>, "though I try to hide it."</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kouetsu', { content: '<h3>Hon\'ami Kōetsu (1558–1637)</h3><p>A man of the arts of the early Edo period, counted as one of the three great calligraphers of the Kan\'ei era.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.makie', { content: '<h3>Maki-e (gold lacquer)</h3><p>A technique of drawing patterns in lacquer and sprinkling gold or silver powder on them before the lacquer dries.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.murakami', { content: '<h3>Emperor Murakami</h3><p>The 62nd emperor, 14th son of Emperor Daigo. He ruled directly, and his reign was later praised as the "Tenryaku era of good rule." He also ordered the compiling of the <em>Gosen Wakashū</em>.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kiyomori', { content: '<h3>Taira no Kiyomori (1118–1181)</h3><p>A warrior and noble of the late Heian period, head of the Taira clan (descended from Emperor Kanmu), and the first warrior to become Grand Minister.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.sonpibunmyaku', { content: '<h3>Sonpi Bunmyaku</h3><p>An early collection of Japanese family trees, compiled in the late Nanboku-chō period (14th century), covering the Minamoto, Taira, Tachibana, Fujiwara and other clans.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shasekishu', { content: '<h3>Shasekishū</h3><p>A collection of Buddhist tales compiled by the monk Mujū, completed in 1283 in the late Kamakura period. Its title, "Sand and Pebbles," means drawing gold from sand and jewels from stones — teaching Buddhism through everyday stories.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mizushidokoro', { content: '<h3>The imperial kitchen office (Mizushidokoro)</h3><p>The office that prepared the emperor\'s meals and the food and drink for court banquets.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.settsunodaisakan', { content: '<h3>Senior secretary of Settsu (Settsu no Daisakan)</h3><p>Settsu Province covered most of north-central Osaka and southeastern Hyōgo. The <em>sakan</em> was the fourth-ranking provincial official; Settsu, a second-rank province, had senior and junior ones.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.goshuuishu', { content: '<h3>Go Shūi Wakashū</h3><p>The fourth imperial anthology of waka, completed in 1086 by order of Emperor Shirakawa and compiled by Fujiwara no Michitoshi. It contains 1,220 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.suenomatsuyama', { content: '<h3>Sue-no-Matsuyama</h3><p>A low hill in Tagajō, Miyagi Prefecture. In waka, "waves crossing Sue-no-Matsuyama" stands for something that can never happen — and so for a lover\'s heart changing.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nashitsubonogonin', { content: '<h3>The Five Men of the Pear Pavilion (Nashitsubo no Gonin)</h3><p>Five poets appointed by Emperor Murakami in 951 to a poetry office in the Shōyōsha hall, where a pear tree grew: Ōnakatomi no Yoshinobu, Minamoto no Shitagō, Kiyohara no Motosuke, Sakanoue no Mochiki and Ki no Tokibumi. They compiled the <em>Gosen Wakashū</em> and worked on reading the <em>Man\'yōshū</em>.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shuuishou', { content: '<h3>Shūishō</h3><p>A private anthology of waka compiled by Fujiwara no Kintō. The imperial <em>Shūi Wakashū</em> was later built on it.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kinuginunouta', { content: '<h3>Morning-after poem (kinuginu no uta)</h3><p>A poem a man sent to a woman the morning after they spent the night together. The name, "robe and robe," is said to come from the two robes, laid together during the night, being parted again.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.biwa', { content: '<h3>Biwa</h3><p>A Japanese lute with a flat, oval wooden body and four or five strings, played with a plectrum. It came to Japan from mainland China around the 7th or 8th century.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.seiryouden', { content: '<h3>Seiryōden</h3><p>One of the halls of the palace in Heian-kyō, where the emperor lived day to day.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_44', { content: '<h3>"Some men, whom now I hate"</h3><p>Porter\'s version wanders from the original, which simply says: if we had never met at all, I would resent neither her nor myself.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shou', { content: '<h3>Shō</h3><p>A wind instrument of <em>gagaku</em> court music: seventeen thin bamboo pipes set in a circle, played held upright. It came from China in the Nara period and was later adapted in Japan.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.ohaguro', { content: '<h3>Ohaguro (blackened teeth)</h3><p>A custom of dyeing the teeth black as a form of makeup, practiced by Heian nobles and in many places around the world.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kazan', { content: '<h3>Emperor Kazan</h3><p>The 65th emperor, first son of Emperor Reizei.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.reizei', { content: '<h3>Emperor Reizei</h3><p>The 63rd emperor, second son of Emperor Murakami.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.dajoudaijin', { content: '<h3>Chancellor of the Realm (Daijō-daijin)</h3><p>The highest post in the Council of State (<em>Daijōkan</em>). It was an honor with no fixed duties, left empty when no suitable person could be found.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.wakadokoro', { content: '<h3>The Office of Poetry (Wakadokoro)</h3><p>A temporary palace office set up to compile an imperial anthology. The first was set up by Emperor Murakami in the Nashitsubo (Pear Pavilion) in 951.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nyogo', { content: '<h3>Nyōgo (consort)</h3><p>A consort of the emperor, ranking below the empress (<em>chūgū</em>) and above a <em>kōi</em>.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kaneie', { content: '<h3>Fujiwara no Kaneie (929–990)</h3><p>A powerful noble of the mid-Heian period, third son of Fujiwara no Morosuke. As grandfather of Emperors Ichijō and Sanjō, he became regent, Kampaku and Chancellor. He was Michinaga\'s father.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.bettou', { content: '<h3>Bettō (head)</h3><p>The person in charge of an office.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.porter_46', { content: '<h3>"So runs the old love-song"</h3><p>Porter\'s addition. The original compares the poet\'s own love directly to the drifting boat.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jokotoba46', { content: '<h3>"Like a boatman…": a preface (jokotoba)</h3><p>The first half, <em>Yura no to o / wataru funabito / kaji o tae</em>, is a <em>jokotoba</em>: the boatman who has lost his oar is an image for "not knowing where I am going" (<em>yukue mo shiranu</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yuranoto', { content: '<h3>The straits of Yura (Yura no to)</h3><p>The mouth of the Yura River in Miyazu, Kyoto Prefecture. <em>To</em> (門) means a place where water flows fast, such as a strait or river mouth.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.hyakushuuta', { content: '<h3>Sets of a hundred poems (hyakushu-uta)</h3><p>A set number of waka, written as a unit of 100. The form is said to have begun with the "hundred poems" in Yoshitada\'s own collection.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tangonojou', { content: '<h3>Secretary of Tango (Tango no Jō)</h3><p>Tango Province covered the Tango Peninsula in northern Kyoto Prefecture. The <em>jō</em> was the third-ranking provincial official.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.enyuu', { content: '<h3>Emperor En\'yū</h3><p>The 64th emperor (reigned 969–984), a son of Emperor Murakami.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shokukokinshu', { content: '<h3>Shoku Kokin Wakashū</h3><p>The eleventh imperial anthology of waka, completed in 1265 in the Kamakura period by order of Retired Emperor Go-Saga. It has about 1,900 poems.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.yaemugura', { content: '<h3>Weeds (yaemugura)</h3><p>In the original, <em>yaemugura</em>: a climbing weed covered in tiny hooks that catch on other plants and on passers-by\'s clothes. In waka, it is the classic sign of a house no one visits.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kawarain', { content: '<h3>Kawara-in</h3><p>The vast mansion of Minamoto no Tōru (the Minister of the Left of Kawara) at Rokujō in Kyoto. Its garden copied the shore of Shiogama in Mutsu, and seawater is said to have been brought from Amagasaki to make salt. After his death it became a temple and fell into ruin after several fires.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.anpou', { content: '<h3>Priest Anpō</h3><p>A Heian poet and great-grandson of Minamoto no Tōru. His family had declined, and he became a monk young and lived at the Kawara-in his great-grandfather had built.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.harima', { content: '<h3>Harima Province</h3><p>An old province covering the southwest of present-day Hyōgo Prefecture.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.kumanomoude', { content: '<h3>The Kumano pilgrimage</h3><p>A pilgrimage to the three great shrines of Kumano (in present-day Wakayama Prefecture). Retired emperors such as Uda and Kazan made it in the Heian period, and it grew popular after Retired Emperor Shirakawa\'s visit in 1116.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tamamakukuzu', { content: '<h3>Kudzu leaves</h3><p>In the original, <em>tamamaku kuzu</em>: kudzu leaves curled up like rolled balls.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jokotoba48', { content: '<h3>"Like the waves…": a preface (jokotoba)</h3><p>The first half, <em>Kaze o itami / iwa utsu nami no</em>, is a <em>jokotoba</em>: waves dashed against a rock by a fierce wind are an image for the poet alone (<em>onore nomi</em>) being broken by love.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.jokotoba49', { content: '<h3>"Like the fire tended by the palace guards": a preface (jokotoba)</h3><p>The first half, <em>Mikakimori / eji no taku hi no</em>, is a <em>jokotoba</em>: the guards\' watch-fires, burning at night and put out by day, are an image for the poet\'s love.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.mikakimori', { content: '<h3>Palace guards (mikakimori)</h3><p>Soldiers who guarded the gates of the palace.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.nakatominokamatari', { content: '<h3>Nakatomi no Kamatari</h3><p>A noble of the Asuka period who carried out the Taika Reforms with Prince Naka no Ōe (later Emperor Tenji). On his deathbed he was given the family name Fujiwara.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.isejingu', { content: '<h3>Ise Shrine</h3><p>The shrine in Ise, Mie Prefecture, with an inner shrine for Amaterasu, the sun goddess and ancestral deity of the imperial family, and an outer shrine for Toyouke. It has long been closely tied to the court.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shingikan', { content: '<h3>The Office of Shrine Affairs (Jingikan)</h3><p>The government office in charge of the court\'s Shinto rites.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.tennenntou', { content: '<h3>Smallpox</h3><p>A highly infectious disease that reached Japan around the 6th century. It caused high fever and a rash all over the body and was often fatal.</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.shinagon', { content: '<h3>The Four Counselors (Shinagon)</h3><p>Four nobles active under Emperor Ichijō: Minamoto no Toshikata, Fujiwara no Kintō, Fujiwara no Tadanobu and Fujiwara no Yukinari, all of whom rose to the rank of counselor (<em>nagon</em>).</p>', allowHTML: true });
tippy('.renga', { content: '<h3>Linked verse (renga)</h3><p>A form of poetry in which several people take turns adding 5-7-5 and 7-7 verses to build one long poem. It began as a game of one person writing the upper verse of a waka and another the lower verse.</p>', allowHTML: true });
